      "why": "unrolled_k is the only family in my set that can plausibly meet the 32 Msps throughput constraint. iterative is strictly 1 result/cycle, so its max throughput is Fmax (typically <32 Msps for a 16-bit CORDIC), making it infeasible for the throughput constraint. unrolled_k with k=4..8 can achieve 4-8 results/cycle, reaching 32+ Msps at moderate Fmax. The sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 us constraint requires all 32 results within 0.44 us of the tick; with k=4, latency is ceil(N/4)+3 cycles, and at ~100 MHz that's ~4-5 cycles = 40-50 ns, well within 440 ns. Accuracy: max_abs_err <= 2^-12 = 0.000244141. With data_width=16, n_iter=14-18, and appropriate guards, CORDIC residual error after N iterations is ~2^(-N) scaled, so N>=14 with W=16 should give ~12+ bits accuracy. I focus data_width at 14-16 (16 is the natural fit for 12-bit accuracy with margin), n_iter 14-18 to balance accuracy vs area, k 2-8 to explore the throughput/area trade-off, and both rounding modes since rounding can add ~1 bit of accuracy for small area cost."
