      "why": "iterative is included as a baseline for the Pareto front mapping, even though it likely fails the throughput_msps >= 32 constraint (1 result/cycle, Fmax probably 80-120 MHz for 16-bit, giving 80-120 Msps... wait, that could actually meet 32 Msps). Reconsidering: iterative at Fmax=100 MHz gives 100 Msps throughput, which exceeds 32 Msps. The constraint is throughput_msps >= 32, and iterative at 100 MHz gives 100 Msps. So iterative CAN meet the throughput constraint. However, the sys_p99_batch_us constraint is the killer: 32 requests must all complete within 0.44 us. With iterative (1 result/cycle), 32 requests take 32 cycles. At 100 MHz, 32 cycles = 320 ns = 0.32 us, which is within 0.44 us. At 80 MHz, 32 cycles = 400 ns = 0.4 us, still within 0.44 us. So iterative might actually be feasible! I include it to map the low-area end of the front. The trade-off is that iterative has the smallest area (shared datapath, no k-way parallelism) but the longest latency per result. For the batch constraint, the total time for 32 sequential results must fit in 0.44 us. This makes iterative a strong candidate for minimum luts_plus_ffs if Fmax is high enough."
    }
