  "rationale": "The spec requires 32 Msps throughput, max_abs_err <= 2^-12, and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 us (all 32 results within 0.44 us of a 1 us tick). I am restricted to iterative and unrolled_k. Both families can potentially meet the throughput constraint: iterative at Fmax >= 32 MHz gives 32+ Msps (1 result/cycle), and unrolled_k with k>=2 gives 2x Fmax Msps. The critical constraint is sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 us: for iterative, 32 sequential results take 32 cycles, so Fmax must be >= 32/0.44us = 72.7 MHz; for unrolled_k with k, 32 results take ceil(32/k) cycles (if pipelined within the k-chain) or 32/k cycles, so the constraint is easier to meet. Accuracy: max_abs_err <= 2^-12 requires sufficient n_iter and data_width. CORDIC error after N iterations is dominated by the residual angle ~2^(-N) and truncation; with W=16 and N>=14, we should achieve 12+ bits. I split the budget 50/50 between the two families: unrolled_k to explore the throughput/area trade-off via k (2-8), and iterative to find the minimum-area feasible point. data_width is focused at 14-16 (16 is the natural width for 12-bit accuracy with Q1.(W-2) format giving 14 fractional bits), n_iter at 14-18 to balance accuracy margin vs area, angle_guard at -2..4 to explore the atan-LUT width trade-off, frac_guard at 0..4 for extra fractional precision, and both rounding modes since rounding can improve accuracy by ~0.5-1 bit at small area cost. The objective is min luts_plus_ffs with max accuracy_bits as a secondary objective, so the Pareto front will trade area against accuracy margin. The selection rule is min luts_plus_ffs, so the final design will be the cheapest feasible point on the front."
}
